# Unit Testing with Jest

Testing is not about proving your code works once — it is about detecting future changes that break something that used to work. A test suite is a safety net that gives you confidence to refactor, add features, and merge pull requests from others without needing to manually re-verify the entire system. Interviewers screen for testing knowledge because untested code in production creates fragile systems and slows teams down.

Jest is the de facto standard test runner for JavaScript and TypeScript. It provides a test runner, assertion library, and mocking framework in a single package — no configuration required for most projects. It runs tests in parallel (in separate worker processes, each with its own module registry), generates coverage reports, and has deep integration with React via `@testing-library/react`. Understanding how Jest works — not just the API surface — makes the difference between tests that catch real bugs and tests that are more brittle than the code they cover.

The most important principle in unit testing is isolation: a unit test should test one thing in isolation from its dependencies. If a test calls a real database, real network endpoint, or relies on system time, it is no longer a unit test — it is an integration test that happens to use Jest as its runner. Isolation is achieved through mocking, and knowing when and how to mock is the core skill this module builds.

---

## Theory & Fundamentals

### What is a Unit Test?

A unit is the smallest testable piece of behavior in your application — typically a single function, method, or module. A unit test verifies that unit's behavior in isolation: given some input (or some preconditions), a specific output (or side effect) is expected. Crucially, "isolation" means the unit's external dependencies (databases, HTTP clients, file system, `Date.now()`) are replaced with controlled fakes during the test.

Why isolation? Real dependencies are slow (network), non-deterministic (time, random), stateful (databases), and may not be available in a CI environment. A mocked dependency is instant, deterministic, and always available.

### The Test Pyramid

The test pyramid is a mental model for distributing test types: many unit tests (fast, isolated, cheap to write and run), fewer integration tests (test multiple components working together), and even fewer end-to-end tests (simulate a full user flow, expensive and slow). Unit tests form the base because they are the fastest feedback loop during development.

### Arrange-Act-Assert

Every well-written test follows three phases: Arrange (set up the preconditions and inputs), Act (invoke the code under test), Assert (verify the output or side effects match expectations). Keeping these phases visually distinct makes tests easy to read and diagnose when they fail.

### Test Names as Documentation

Test names should describe behavior, not implementation. "returns 0 for empty array" describes what the function should do. "loops through array and checks each element" describes how, which breaks when the implementation changes (even if the behavior is correct). Tests named as behavior specifications serve as living documentation that stays in sync with the code.

---

## Core Concepts / Techniques

### describe / it / test Structure

```javascript
// describe groups related tests; it/test declares a single expected behavior
describe('calculateDiscount', () => {
  it('applies 10% discount for silver tier users', () => {
    const result = calculateDiscount(100, 'silver');
    expect(result).toBe(90);
  });

  it('applies 20% discount for gold tier users', () => {
    const result = calculateDiscount(100, 'gold');
    expect(result).toBe(80);
  });

  it('returns full price for unknown tiers', () => {
    const result = calculateDiscount(100, 'unknown');
    expect(result).toBe(100);
  });

  it('throws for negative prices', () => {
    expect(() => calculateDiscount(-10, 'silver')).toThrow('Price must be positive');
  });
});
```

### Assertions

```javascript
// Primitive equality — uses ===
expect(result).toBe(42);
expect(result).not.toBe(0);

// Deep structural equality for objects and arrays — does NOT use ===
expect(user).toEqual({ id: 1, name: 'Prasanth', active: true });
expect(tags).toEqual(['js', 'sql', 'redis']);

// Truthiness matchers
expect(value).toBeTruthy();
expect(value).toBeFalsy();
expect(value).toBeNull();
expect(value).toBeUndefined();
expect(value).toBeDefined();

// Number comparisons
expect(result).toBeGreaterThan(0);
expect(result).toBeLessThanOrEqual(100);
expect(result).toBeCloseTo(3.14, 2); // floating-point safe comparison

// String matchers
expect(message).toMatch(/error/i);         // regex match
expect(message).toContain('required');     // substring

// Array matchers
expect(items).toHaveLength(3);
expect(items).toContain('apple');
expect(items).toContainEqual({ id: 1 });  // deep equality for objects in array

// Error assertions
expect(() => fn(invalidInput)).toThrow();
expect(() => fn(invalidInput)).toThrow('specific message');
expect(() => fn(invalidInput)).toThrow(ValidationError); // error class
```

### Mocking with jest.fn() and jest.spyOn()

```javascript
// jest.fn(): create a standalone mock function
const mockSendEmail = jest.fn();
mockSendEmail.mockReturnValue({ sent: true });
mockSendEmail.mockReturnValueOnce({ sent: false }); // only for the FIRST call
mockSendEmail.mockResolvedValue({ sent: true });     // async — returns a promise

// Assert on how the mock was called
expect(mockSendEmail).toHaveBeenCalled();
expect(mockSendEmail).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2);
expect(mockSendEmail).toHaveBeenCalledWith('user@example.com', expect.any(String));
expect(mockSendEmail).not.toHaveBeenCalled();

// jest.spyOn(): wrap an existing module method to observe/control it
import * as emailService from './emailService.js';
const spy = jest.spyOn(emailService, 'send');
spy.mockResolvedValue({ sent: true });

// After the test, restore the original implementation
spy.mockRestore();
```

### Mocking Modules

```javascript
// Mock an entire module — every import of this module in the test file
// gets the mocked version
jest.mock('./database.js', () => ({
  query: jest.fn(),
  getUser: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ id: 1, name: 'Prasanth' })
}));

// Mock a node module
jest.mock('nodemailer', () => ({
  createTransport: jest.fn().mockReturnValue({
    sendMail: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ messageId: 'test-id' })
  })
}));

// Accessing the mock in tests to assert or configure per-test behavior
import { query } from './database.js';
query.mockResolvedValueOnce([{ id: 1, name: 'Prasanth' }]);
```

### Setup and Teardown

```javascript
describe('UserService', () => {
  let service;
  let mockDb;

  beforeAll(() => {
    // Runs once before all tests in this describe block
    // Good for expensive setup that all tests share
    console.log('Setting up test suite');
  });

  afterAll(() => {
    // Runs once after all tests — clean up shared resources
    console.log('Tearing down test suite');
  });

  beforeEach(() => {
    // Runs before EACH test — provides a fresh, clean state
    mockDb = { query: jest.fn() };
    service = new UserService(mockDb);
  });

  afterEach(() => {
    // Runs after EACH test — clear mocks so tests don't interfere
    jest.clearAllMocks();
    // clearAllMocks: clears call counts and return values
    // resetAllMocks: also resets mock implementations
    // restoreAllMocks: restores spies to original implementations
  });

  it('creates a user', async () => {
    mockDb.query.mockResolvedValue({ rows: [{ id: 'uuid-1', name: 'Alice' }] });
    const user = await service.createUser({ name: 'Alice' });
    expect(user.id).toBeDefined();
    expect(mockDb.query).toHaveBeenCalledWith(
      expect.stringContaining('INSERT'),
      expect.arrayContaining(['Alice'])
    );
  });
});
```

---

## Code Examples

### Testing an Async Service Function

```javascript
// src/services/orderService.js
export async function placeOrder(userId, items) {
  if (!items.length) throw new Error('Order must have at least one item');

  const total = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity, 0);
  const order = await db.createOrder({ userId, total });
  await emailService.sendOrderConfirmation(order);
  return order;
}

// src/services/orderService.test.js
import { placeOrder } from './orderService.js';
import * as db from '../db.js';
import * as emailService from './emailService.js';

jest.mock('../db.js');
jest.mock('./emailService.js');

describe('placeOrder', () => {
  const mockOrder = { id: 'order-1', userId: 'user-1', total: 50 };

  beforeEach(() => {
    db.createOrder.mockResolvedValue(mockOrder);
    emailService.sendOrderConfirmation.mockResolvedValue(undefined);
  });

  afterEach(() => jest.clearAllMocks());

  it('calculates total and creates an order', async () => {
    const items = [
      { price: 25, quantity: 1 },
      { price: 12.50, quantity: 2 }
    ];

    const result = await placeOrder('user-1', items);

    expect(db.createOrder).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ userId: 'user-1', total: 50 });
    expect(result).toEqual(mockOrder);
  });

  it('sends a confirmation email after creating the order', async () => {
    await placeOrder('user-1', [{ price: 10, quantity: 1 }]);
    expect(emailService.sendOrderConfirmation).toHaveBeenCalledWith(mockOrder);
  });

  it('throws when the items array is empty', async () => {
    await expect(placeOrder('user-1', [])).rejects.toThrow('Order must have at least one item');
    expect(db.createOrder).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  });
});
```

### Testing with Fake Timers

```javascript
// Testing code that depends on time
import { generateSessionId, isSessionExpired } from './session.js';

describe('isSessionExpired', () => {
  beforeEach(() => jest.useFakeTimers());
  afterEach(() => jest.useRealTimers());

  it('returns false for a fresh session', () => {
    const session = { createdAt: Date.now(), ttlMs: 3600_000 };
    expect(isSessionExpired(session)).toBe(false);
  });

  it('returns true after the TTL elapses', () => {
    const session = { createdAt: Date.now(), ttlMs: 3600_000 };
    jest.advanceTimersByTime(3600_001); // advance 1 hour + 1ms
    expect(isSessionExpired(session)).toBe(true);
  });
});
```

---

## Best Practices

- **Test behavior, not implementation** — assert on outputs and observable side effects, not on internal state, private methods, or which internal functions were called. Tests that couple to implementation break when you refactor (even correctly).
- **Each test should have exactly one reason to fail** — testing multiple behaviors in a single `it` block makes failures ambiguous; one assertion per logical behavior (not per `expect` call) is the rule.
- **Use `beforeEach` for state setup, not `beforeAll`** — shared state across tests through `beforeAll` causes tests to interfere with each other; `beforeEach` guarantees a clean slate for every test.
- **Clear mocks between tests** — call `jest.clearAllMocks()` in `afterEach` to prevent call count accumulation and return value leakage between tests.
- **Use `toEqual` for objects and arrays, `toBe` for primitives** — `toBe` uses reference equality (`===`); two separately created objects that look identical will not `toBe` equal.
- **Name tests as sentences describing expected behavior** — "returns an empty array when no items match the filter" is useful documentation; "test filter function" is not.
- **Treat a test failure as a first-class event** — a failing test is telling you something real: either the code has a bug, the test has a bug, or the requirements changed. Deleting or skipping a failing test is the worst of the three options.

---

## Common Interview Questions

**Q: What is the difference between a unit test and an integration test?**
A: A unit test tests a single function or module in isolation, with all external dependencies replaced by mocks. An integration test tests multiple components working together — for example, a service function calling a real database. Unit tests are faster and more targeted; integration tests are slower but catch problems that only appear when real components interact.

**Q: What is the difference between toBe and toEqual in Jest?**
A: `toBe` uses strict reference equality (`===`) — two separately created objects with identical contents will not pass `toBe`. `toEqual` performs deep structural comparison — two objects with the same keys and values pass `toEqual` even if they are different references. Use `toBe` for primitives and `toEqual` for objects and arrays.

**Q: What is jest.fn() and why do we use it?**
A: `jest.fn()` creates a mock function that records how it was called (arguments, call count, return values) without executing any real logic. We use it to replace real dependencies (database calls, network requests) so tests are fast, deterministic, and do not depend on external services being available.

**Q: What is the difference between jest.clearAllMocks(), jest.resetAllMocks(), and jest.restoreAllMocks()?**
A: `clearAllMocks` clears mock call history and results but keeps implementations. `resetAllMocks` also removes any mock implementations set with `mockReturnValue` etc. `restoreAllMocks` goes furthest — it restores spies created with `jest.spyOn` back to the original implementation. Call the appropriate one in `afterEach` based on what you want preserved.

**Q: How do you test a function that uses setTimeout or Date.now()?**
A: Use `jest.useFakeTimers()` to replace real timer functions with Jest-controlled fakes. Then use `jest.advanceTimersByTime(ms)` or `jest.runAllTimers()` to simulate the passage of time without actual waiting. Fake timers also affect `Date.now()` when Jest replaces it.

**Q: What is code coverage and what are its limitations?**
A: Code coverage measures the percentage of lines, branches, functions, and statements executed by tests. It reveals which code has zero test coverage. Its limitation is that coverage says nothing about assertion quality — a test that executes a function but only asserts `expect(result).toBeDefined()` adds coverage without verifying correct behavior.

**Q: How would you mock a module that makes HTTP requests in tests?**
A: Use `jest.mock('moduleName')` to replace the module entirely, then configure the mock with `mockResolvedValue` or `mockReturnValue` to return controlled responses. For HTTP libraries, `jest.spyOn(axios, 'get').mockResolvedValue({ data: {...} })` works well. For fetch-based code in integration-style tests, consider `msw` (Mock Service Worker) which intercepts at the network level.

**Q: What is test isolation and why does it matter?**
A: Test isolation means each test is independent — its outcome does not depend on whether other tests ran before it or what order they ran in. It matters because non-isolated tests produce unreliable results: they can pass when run in one order and fail in another, making failures intermittent and hard to diagnose.

---

## Practice Exercises

1. **Pure function testing**: Write tests for a `calculateCartTotal(items, discountCode)` function that handles: empty carts, invalid discount codes, valid percentage discounts, and a maximum discount cap. Write at least one test for each behavior branch.

2. **Async service with mocks**: Write tests for a `registerUser(email, password)` function that validates email format, hashes the password using a `bcrypt` module, and saves to a database module. Mock both `bcrypt` and the database module. Test the happy path, duplicate email error, and invalid email validation.

3. **Timer-based function**: Implement and test a `debounce(fn, delayMs)` function using fake timers. Test that: the function is not called immediately, it is called after the delay elapses, multiple rapid calls result in only one invocation (the last one), and the delay resets on each new call.

4. **Error propagation**: Write tests for a middleware function that calls an async route handler and forwards any thrown error to Express's `next(err)`. Test that errors are correctly forwarded and that `next` is not called when the handler succeeds.

5. **Retrofit existing tests**: Take a function you have already written without tests and add a test suite for it. Identify what dependencies need to be mocked, what edge cases exist, and what the failure behavior should be. Aim for meaningful assertions, not just coverage.

---

## Key Takeaways

- Unit tests verify isolated behavior — every external dependency (database, network, time) must be replaced with a controlled mock so tests are fast, deterministic, and environment-independent.
- `describe` groups related tests; `it`/`test` declares a single expected behavior; test names should be readable sentences describing what the code should do, not how it does it.
- `toBe` uses reference equality; `toEqual` uses deep structural equality — use `toEqual` for objects and arrays.
- `jest.fn()` creates mock functions that record calls; `jest.mock('module')` replaces entire modules; `jest.spyOn` wraps existing methods to observe or override them.
- `beforeEach` provides clean state before every test, preventing shared state between tests from causing interference; `afterEach` with `jest.clearAllMocks()` resets mock state.
- Code coverage is a signal for gaps, not a quality metric — 100% coverage with weak assertions is worse than 70% coverage with assertions that verify real behavior.
- Tests that couple to implementation (asserting internal state or which private methods were called) break on correct refactors; tests that assert on observable output are resilient to implementation changes.
- Fake timers (`jest.useFakeTimers`) allow testing time-dependent code without real delays — `jest.advanceTimersByTime` simulates the passage of time synchronously.

lessons.3.2:
# Component & Integration Testing with React Testing Library

React Testing Library (RTL) was created in response to the pattern of testing React component internals — checking component state, calling instance methods, and querying by CSS class name. Tests written that way pass when the component works but also break when the component is refactored correctly, because they are coupled to the implementation rather than the behavior. RTL's answer is to test components the way a user interacts with them: by what appears in the DOM, by what accessible roles elements have, and by what happens when a user types or clicks.

The core philosophy is stated directly in RTL's documentation: "The more your tests resemble the way your software is used, the more confidence they can give you." This has a concrete implication for how you write tests: the test should not know whether a component manages state with `useState`, `useReducer`, or a state management library. The test should only know what renders in the DOM as a result of user actions.

An important secondary effect of RTL's query priority — preferring queries like `getByRole` and `getByLabelText` over `getByTestId` — is that it acts as an implicit accessibility audit. If you cannot query a button by its accessible role and name, that is evidence the button is not properly accessible to a screen reader user either. Writing RTL tests and making them pass often improves accessibility as a side effect.

---

## Theory & Fundamentals

### Behavior Testing vs. Implementation Testing

Implementation testing: asserting that a specific function was called internally, that state has a specific value, that a specific class name is present. These tests break when you refactor even if the user-facing behavior is unchanged.

Behavior testing: asserting that a specific text appears, a specific button is enabled or disabled, a specific error message shows after an invalid action. These tests survive refactors that do not change user-visible behavior.

The rule: if you can freely change the internals of a component — replacing `useState` with a custom hook, changing a class name, restructuring the component tree — and your tests still pass, they are behavioral. If those internal changes break your tests, they are implementation tests.

### Query Priority

RTL provides multiple query functions and recommends a priority order:

1. **Role queries** (`getByRole`) — queries by ARIA role (button, textbox, heading, etc.) with an optional `name` that matches the accessible label. The most semantically meaningful and accessibility-aligned.
2. **Label queries** (`getByLabelText`) — queries a form input by its associated label. For form fields, this is often the most natural query.
3. **Placeholder/text** (`getByPlaceholderText`, `getByText`) — visible text content, useful for non-input elements.
4. **Display value** (`getByDisplayValue`) — current value of a form element.
5. **Alt text** (`getByAltText`) — for images.
6. **Title** (`getByTitle`) — for elements with title attributes.
7. **Test ID** (`getByTestId`) — escape hatch; use only when no semantic query fits.

The `getBy*` prefix throws if zero or more than one element matches. `queryBy*` returns null on no match (does not throw). `findBy*` returns a promise and retries until the element appears or times out — essential for async rendering.

### Async Rendering

React state updates, data fetching, and effects are asynchronous. After simulating a user action, the DOM may not update synchronously. RTL provides:
- `findBy*`: waits (with retries) until the element appears. Use for elements that appear after async operations.
- `waitFor`: retries an assertion until it passes or times out. Use when the element already exists but you are waiting for a change.
- `waitForElementToBeRemoved`: waits for an element to disappear from the DOM.

---

## Core Concepts / Techniques

### Rendering Components

```jsx
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';

// render() mounts the component in a jsdom environment
// screen provides queries against the rendered DOM

test('renders the product name and price', () => {
  render(<ProductCard name="Coffee Mug" price={12.99} />);

  // getByRole + name option: matches the heading with that text
  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { name: /coffee mug/i })).toBeInTheDocument();

  // getByText: find by visible text content
  expect(screen.getByText('$12.99')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

### Querying the DOM

```jsx
// getByRole examples — most preferred query type
screen.getByRole('button', { name: /submit/i });   // button with accessible name "Submit"
screen.getByRole('textbox', { name: /email/i });   // input labeled "Email"
screen.getByRole('checkbox', { name: /agree/i });
screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 2 });          // <h2> elements specifically
screen.getByRole('link', { name: /view details/i });
screen.getByRole('img', { name: /product photo/i }); // img with alt text

// getByLabelText: matches <input> associated with <label>
screen.getByLabelText(/email address/i);
screen.getByLabelText(/password/i);

// queryBy: returns null instead of throwing when not found
// Good for asserting something is NOT in the DOM
expect(screen.queryByText(/error/i)).not.toBeInTheDocument();

// findBy: async, retries until element appears (returns a Promise)
const successMessage = await screen.findByText(/order placed/i);
expect(successMessage).toBeInTheDocument();

// getAllBy, queryAllBy, findAllBy: return arrays of matching elements
const buttons = screen.getAllByRole('button');
expect(buttons).toHaveLength(3);
```

### User Events

```jsx
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
// userEvent simulates real browser interactions (full event sequence)
// Prefer userEvent over fireEvent — it is more realistic

test('submits the login form with valid credentials', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();   // creates a userEvent instance
  const mockSubmit = jest.fn();

  render(<LoginForm onSubmit={mockSubmit} />);

  // type simulates a real user typing character by character
  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i), 'test@example.com');
  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/password/i), 'secret123');

  // click fires mousedown, mouseup, click events in sequence
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /log in/i }));

  expect(mockSubmit).toHaveBeenCalledWith({
    email: 'test@example.com',
    password: 'secret123'
  });
});

// Other userEvent interactions
await user.clear(screen.getByLabelText(/search/i));  // clears input value
await user.selectOptions(screen.getByRole('combobox'), ['option-1']);
await user.keyboard('{Enter}');                       // keyboard events
await user.tab();                                     // focus navigation
```

### Async Utilities and waitFor

```jsx
test('shows a loading state then displays fetched data', async () => {
  render(<UserProfile userId="42" />);

  // Loading state should be visible immediately
  expect(screen.getByText(/loading/i)).toBeInTheDocument();

  // findBy waits for the element to appear (default 1000ms timeout)
  expect(await screen.findByText('Prasanth Kumar Jha')).toBeInTheDocument();

  // Loading state should be gone after data loads
  expect(screen.queryByText(/loading/i)).not.toBeInTheDocument();
});

// waitFor: retry an assertion until it passes
test('disables the button while submitting', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<ContactForm />);

  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /send/i }));

  // Button becomes disabled immediately after click
  await waitFor(() => {
    expect(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /send/i })).toBeDisabled();
  });
});
```

---

## Code Examples

### Testing a Form with Validation

```jsx
// src/components/RegistrationForm.test.jsx
import { render, screen, waitFor } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import RegistrationForm from './RegistrationForm';

describe('RegistrationForm', () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();

  async function fillAndSubmit({ email, password, confirmPassword }) {
    if (email)           await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i), email);
    if (password)        await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/^password/i), password);
    if (confirmPassword) await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/confirm password/i), confirmPassword);
    await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /register/i }));
  }

  beforeEach(() => render(<RegistrationForm />));

  it('renders all form fields', () => {
    expect(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
    expect(screen.getByLabelText(/^password/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
    expect(screen.getByLabelText(/confirm password/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
    expect(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /register/i })).toBeInTheDocument();
  });

  it('shows an error when email is invalid', async () => {
    await fillAndSubmit({ email: 'not-an-email', password: 'abc123', confirmPassword: 'abc123' });
    expect(await screen.findByText(/valid email/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
  });

  it('shows an error when passwords do not match', async () => {
    await fillAndSubmit({ email: 'a@b.com', password: 'abc123', confirmPassword: 'xyz789' });
    expect(await screen.findByText(/passwords must match/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
  });

  it('submits successfully with valid inputs', async () => {
    await fillAndSubmit({ email: 'a@b.com', password: 'secure123', confirmPassword: 'secure123' });
    expect(await screen.findByText(/registration successful/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
  });
});
```

### Mocking API Calls with MSW

```javascript
// src/mocks/handlers.js
import { http, HttpResponse } from 'msw';

export const handlers = [
  http.get('/api/users/:id', ({ params }) => {
    return HttpResponse.json({
      id: params.id,
      name: 'Prasanth Kumar Jha',
      email: 'prasanth@example.com'
    });
  }),

  http.post('/api/orders', async ({ request }) => {
    const body = await request.json();
    return HttpResponse.json({ id: 'order-1', ...body }, { status: 201 });
  }),
];

// src/setupTests.js
import { setupServer } from 'msw/node';
import { handlers } from './mocks/handlers';

export const server = setupServer(...handlers);
beforeAll(() => server.listen());
afterEach(() => server.resetHandlers()); // reset per-test overrides
afterAll(() => server.close());

// In a specific test, override the default handler
test('shows error message on API failure', async () => {
  server.use(
    http.get('/api/users/:id', () =>
      HttpResponse.json({ message: 'User not found' }, { status: 404 })
    )
  );

  render(<UserProfile userId="99" />);
  expect(await screen.findByText(/user not found/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

### Integration Test: Full User Flow

```jsx
// Test a complete "add to cart and checkout" flow
// This is an integration test — it tests multiple components working together
import { render, screen, within } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import App from './App';

test('user can add a product to cart and view the updated cart count', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<App />);

  // Navigate to a product
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('link', { name: /coffee mug/i }));

  // Cart count starts at 0
  const cartIcon = screen.getByRole('button', { name: /cart/i });
  expect(within(cartIcon).getByText('0')).toBeInTheDocument();

  // Add to cart
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /add to cart/i }));

  // Cart count updates to 1
  expect(within(cartIcon).getByText('1')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

---

## Best Practices

- **Query by role first**, then label, then text — reach for `getByTestId` only when no accessible query fits; using role queries first ensures your components have proper accessibility structure.
- **Use `userEvent` instead of `fireEvent`** — `userEvent` simulates the full browser event sequence a real user interaction triggers; `fireEvent` fires only the named event and misses surrounding events that application code may depend on.
- **Use `findBy*` for elements that appear after async operations** — never use `getBy*` followed by manual waits; RTL's async queries handle retry logic correctly with appropriate timeouts.
- **Mock at the network boundary with MSW** rather than mocking module imports in RTL tests — MSW intercepts actual fetch/XHR calls, making tests closer to what the browser actually does.
- **Test user flows, not component trees** — a test that clicks through a real user action (fills a form, submits, sees a result) is more valuable than a test that renders a component and checks its props.
- **Avoid snapshot tests for component logic** — snapshot tests catch any DOM change (including meaningless refactors) and require constant manual verification; prefer explicit assertions about specific text or roles.
- **Wrap provider setup in a custom render utility** — create a `renderWithProviders(ui, options)` wrapper that includes all necessary context providers (Router, Store, Theme) so individual tests don't need boilerplate.

---

## Common Interview Questions

**Q: What is the guiding philosophy of React Testing Library?**
A: Test components the way users interact with them — through visible text, accessible roles, and simulated user events — rather than by querying internal state or implementation details. This produces tests that are resilient to refactoring and serve as meaningful documentation of expected behavior.

**Q: What is the difference between getBy, queryBy, and findBy queries?**
A: `getBy*` throws if zero or more than one element matches — use it when the element must be present. `queryBy*` returns null when no element matches instead of throwing — use it to assert an element is NOT present. `findBy*` is async and retries until the element appears or a timeout elapses — use it for elements that appear after async operations like data fetching.

**Q: Why is getByRole preferred over getByTestId?**
A: `getByRole` queries by ARIA role and accessible name, which is how assistive technology (screen readers) experiences the page. A component that passes `getByRole` tests is also accessible to users with disabilities. `getByTestId` requires adding `data-testid` attributes to the DOM, which are implementation details that add noise to the HTML and couple tests to markup that has no semantic meaning.

**Q: What is the difference between userEvent and fireEvent?**
A: `userEvent` simulates realistic browser interactions — typing triggers focus, keydown, input, keyup, and change events in sequence. `fireEvent` dispatches only the single specified event. `userEvent` is preferred because it tests the full event chain that application code may depend on; `fireEvent` can produce false-positive test results when event handlers depend on the full sequence.

**Q: How do you test a component that makes an API call on mount?**
A: Mock the API at the network level using MSW (Mock Service Worker), which intercepts fetch/XHR requests and returns controlled responses. Render the component, wait for the async data to appear using `findBy*`, and assert on the rendered content. This approach tests the component's network integration without hitting a real server.

**Q: What is the within utility and when is it useful?**
A: `within(element)` scopes subsequent queries to within a specific DOM element, rather than searching the entire document. Use it when the same text or role appears multiple times in the document and you need to query within a specific section — for example, finding a "Delete" button within a specific list item when multiple list items each have their own "Delete" button.

**Q: How would you test that an error message appears when an API call fails?**
A: Using MSW, override the default handler for the specific test to return an error response. Render the component, trigger the action that initiates the API call (or let it fire on mount), then use `findByText` to wait for the error message to appear in the DOM. Assert that the error message text is present and that the normal success content is absent.

---

## Practice Exercises

1. **Form with validation**: Write tests for a login form component. Test that: the form renders with email and password fields, an error message appears when the email is left empty and the form is submitted, an error appears for a password under 8 characters, and a success callback is called with the correct values when the form is valid.

2. **Async data fetching**: Test a `ProductList` component that fetches products from `/api/products` on mount. Write tests for: the loading state while fetching, the product names appearing after fetch succeeds, and an error message when the fetch returns a 500. Use MSW to mock the API.

3. **Interactive counter**: Test a `Counter` component with increment, decrement, and reset buttons. Test that the initial value displays correctly, each button changes the count as expected, the count cannot go below zero (if that is a requirement), and the reset button returns the count to zero.

4. **Conditional rendering**: Test a `UserMenu` component that shows a "Log in" button when the user is not authenticated and shows the user's name with a "Log out" button when they are. Use context or props to control the authentication state. Test both states and the transition between them.

5. **Integration flow**: Test a shopping cart flow that spans two components: a `ProductCard` with an "Add to cart" button and a `CartSummary` that displays the item count and total. Test that clicking "Add to cart" updates the count in `CartSummary` and that removing an item decrements it.

---

## Key Takeaways

- RTL's philosophy: test behavior users can see (text, roles, enabled/disabled state) rather than implementation details (state values, class names, internal function calls).
- Query priority: `getByRole` first (most accessible and semantic), then `getByLabelText`, then `getByText`, then `getByTestId` as a last resort.
- `getBy*` throws if absent; `queryBy*` returns null; `findBy*` is async and retries — match the variant to whether the element is expected to be immediately present, optionally absent, or yet to appear.
- `userEvent` simulates the full browser event sequence; `fireEvent` fires only the single named event — prefer `userEvent` for accurate simulation of user interaction.
- Mock APIs at the network level with MSW rather than mocking imports — this tests the component's actual fetch/XHR code path and produces more realistic tests.
- Wrap provider setup in a custom `renderWithProviders` utility so tests stay focused on behavior, not on boilerplate context setup.
- Async queries (`findBy*`, `waitFor`) are the correct tool for anything that involves React state updates, effects, or data fetching — never use `getBy*` and hope the DOM has already updated.
- A test that cannot be written without querying internal state or bypassing the DOM is often a signal that the component's public interface needs rethinking, not that the test tool is wrong.

lessons.8.4:
# LLM APIs & Integration

Working with LLM APIs in production is not the same as querying them in a notebook. Production use requires handling streaming responses, implementing retry logic with exponential backoff, managing rate limits and costs, structuring multi-turn conversations correctly, and using function calling to bridge the model's natural language output to your application's typed interfaces. These are the skills that distinguish an engineer who has shipped an AI feature from one who has only experimented with one.

This unit covers the OpenAI and Anthropic APIs in practical depth: the message structure, streaming, tool/function calling with structured schemas, error handling patterns, and the TypeScript/JavaScript integration patterns relevant to full-stack applications. The concepts apply across providers — the APIs differ in naming and some defaults, but the architectural patterns are identical.

Understanding function calling is the most important practical skill in this unit. It is the mechanism that connects an LLM's language capabilities to your application's actual actions — checking databases, calling other services, returning typed data. Everything in Unit 8.6 (agents) and Unit 8.8 (MCP) builds on the function calling pattern established here.

## Theory & Fundamentals

**The Messages API structure**: Modern LLM APIs use a structured conversation format rather than raw text completion. A request contains a list of messages with roles (`system`, `user`, `assistant`), enabling multi-turn conversations where context is maintained by including the full message history. The system message sets persistent instructions (persona, constraints, output format); user messages are human turns; assistant messages are the model's prior responses. This structure is what makes "conversational AI" work — the model sees the entire conversation each time.

**Tokens, context, and cost**: Every API call is metered in tokens — input tokens (everything in your messages array) plus output tokens (the model's response). Costs differ between providers and models; input tokens are typically cheaper than output tokens. Context length is the shared budget for input + output tokens. For long conversations, the message history can grow to fill the entire context window — you must implement truncation or summarization strategies to prevent context overflow in long-running applications.

**Streaming**: By default, the API returns the complete response after generation is finished, causing a delay proportional to output length. Streaming returns tokens as they are generated — users see the response appearing in real time. For web applications this is a significant UX improvement, especially for long RAG answers or complex reasoning. Server-Sent Events (SSE) is the standard transport for streaming in HTTP applications.

**Function calling / tool use**: The API extension that allows the model to request execution of a defined function rather than just generating text. The model decides when a function call is appropriate, generates structured JSON arguments, and your code executes the actual function and returns the result. This creates a structured bridge between natural language and application code, enabling agents that can query databases, call APIs, and take actions.

**Rate limits and retry strategy**: LLM APIs enforce rate limits at the requests-per-minute (RPM) and tokens-per-minute (TPM) level. Exceeding them returns a 429 error. The correct handling is exponential backoff with jitter: retry after an increasing delay (2s, 4s, 8s...) with randomized jitter to avoid thundering-herd retries when multiple clients hit the limit simultaneously. Never retry immediately on a 429 — it will continue to fail.

## Core Concepts / Techniques

**Basic chat completion (Python)**:
```python
from anthropic import Anthropic

client = Anthropic()

def chat(messages: list[dict], system: str = "") -> str:
    response = client.messages.create(
        model="claude-opus-4-5",
        max_tokens=1024,
        system=system,
        messages=messages,
    )
    return response.content[0].text

# Single turn
answer = chat(
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Explain backpropagation in one paragraph."}],
    system="You are a concise technical instructor."
)

# Multi-turn: maintain history manually
history = []
def send(user_msg: str) -> str:
    history.append({"role": "user", "content": user_msg})
    reply = chat(history, system="You are a helpful assistant.")
    history.append({"role": "assistant", "content": reply})
    return reply
```

**Streaming (Python)**:
```python
with client.messages.stream(
    model="claude-opus-4-5",
    max_tokens=512,
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Write a haiku about neural networks."}],
) as stream:
    for text in stream.text_stream:
        print(text, end="", flush=True)
    print()  # newline after done
```

**Function calling (OpenAI SDK, Python)**:
```python
from openai import OpenAI
import json

client = OpenAI()

tools = [
    {
        "type": "function",
        "function": {
            "name": "get_user_orders",
            "description": "Retrieve the most recent orders for a user by their ID.",
            "parameters": {
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                    "user_id":  {"type": "string", "description": "The user's UUID"},
                    "limit":    {"type": "integer", "description": "Number of orders to return", "default": 5},
                },
                "required": ["user_id"],
            },
        },
    }
]

def get_user_orders(user_id: str, limit: int = 5) -> list[dict]:
    # Real implementation would query a database
    return [{"order_id": f"ORD-{i}", "total": 49.99 + i} for i in range(limit)]

def run_with_tools(user_message: str) -> str:
    messages = [{"role": "user", "content": user_message}]
    while True:
        response = client.chat.completions.create(
            model="gpt-4o",
            messages=messages,
            tools=tools,
            tool_choice="auto",
        )
        msg = response.choices[0].message

        if not msg.tool_calls:
            return msg.content   # no more tool calls — final answer

        # Execute all requested tool calls
        messages.append(msg)
        for tc in msg.tool_calls:
            fn_name = tc.function.name
            fn_args = json.loads(tc.function.arguments)
            result  = get_user_orders(**fn_args) if fn_name == "get_user_orders" else {}
            messages.append({
                "role":         "tool",
                "tool_call_id": tc.id,
                "content":      json.dumps(result),
            })

print(run_with_tools("What are the latest orders for user abc-123?"))
```

## Code Examples

```typescript
// TypeScript/Next.js: streaming chat endpoint with tool calling
// app/api/chat/route.ts

import Anthropic from "@anthropic-ai/sdk";
import { NextRequest } from "next/server";

const client = new Anthropic();

// Tool definitions
const tools: Anthropic.Tool[] = [
  {
    name: "search_products",
    description: "Search the product catalog by keyword or category.",
    input_schema: {
      type: "object" as const,
      properties: {
        query:    { type: "string",  description: "Search terms" },
        category: { type: "string",  description: "Optional product category filter" },
        limit:    { type: "integer", description: "Max results", default: 5 },
      },
      required: ["query"],
    },
  },
];

// Simulated tool execution
function searchProducts(query: string, category?: string, limit = 5) {
  return [
    { id: "P001", name: `Widget matching "${query}"`, price: 29.99, category: "Hardware" },
    { id: "P002", name: `Gadget for "${query}"`,     price: 49.99, category: "Electronics" },
  ].slice(0, limit);
}

export async function POST(req: NextRequest) {
  const { messages } = await req.json();

  // Create a streaming response using SSE
  const encoder = new TextEncoder();
  const stream  = new TransformStream();
  const writer  = stream.writable.getWriter();

  const writeChunk = async (data: string) => {
    await writer.write(encoder.encode(`data: ${JSON.stringify({ text: data })}\n\n`));
  };

  (async () => {
    try {
      let currentMessages = [...messages];

      // Agentic loop: repeat until no more tool calls
      while (true) {
        const response = await client.messages.create({
          model:      "claude-opus-4-5",
          max_tokens: 1024,
          system:     "You are a helpful product assistant. Use the search_products tool to look up items.",
          messages:   currentMessages,
          tools,
        });

        // Stream text blocks to the client
        for (const block of response.content) {
          if (block.type === "text") {
            // Stream word-by-word for demo purposes
            for (const word of block.text.split(" ")) {
              await writeChunk(word + " ");
            }
          }
        }

        if (response.stop_reason !== "tool_use") break;

        // Execute tool calls
        const toolResults: Anthropic.MessageParam = {
          role: "user",
          content: response.content
            .filter((b): b is Anthropic.ToolUseBlock => b.type === "tool_use")
            .map((toolUse) => {
              const input = toolUse.input as { query: string; category?: string; limit?: number };
              const result = searchProducts(input.query, input.category, input.limit);
              return {
                type:        "tool_result" as const,
                tool_use_id: toolUse.id,
                content:     JSON.stringify(result),
              };
            }),
        };

        currentMessages = [
          ...currentMessages,
          { role: "assistant" as const, content: response.content },
          toolResults,
        ];
      }
    } catch (err) {
      await writeChunk(`\n[Error: ${(err as Error).message}]`);
    } finally {
      await writer.close();
    }
  })();

  return new Response(stream.readable, {
    headers: {
      "Content-Type":  "text/event-stream",
      "Cache-Control": "no-cache",
      Connection:      "keep-alive",
    },
  });
}
```

